NAME
       automount - the program behind the autofs service

SYNOPSIS
       systemctl enable --now autofs

DESCRIPTION
       automount is the daemon of the autofs package. It watches mount points
       listed in its maps and mounts a file system (usually an NFS export) the
       first time someone goes into the directory, then unmounts it again
       after a while without use. You do not run it by hand: start the autofs
       service instead, and reload or restart it after you change a map.

       The master map /etc/auto.master (and files in /etc/auto.master.d/
       ending in .autofs) names each base directory and the map that
       describes it:

           /shares   /etc/auto.shares

       A map lists keys (names inside the base directory), mount options and
       what to mount:

           work   -rw,sync   nfs.lab.example.com:/exports/shared

       With that, ls /shares/work mounts the export on /shares/work.

       A key of * with & in the location makes a wildcard map: every name you
       ask for is looked up on the server under the same name, which is how
       home directories for network users are usually set up:

           *   -rw   nfs.lab.example.com:/exports/home/&

       A master map line of /- (a direct map) uses full paths as keys
       instead.

EXAMPLES
       Set up /shares/work and start the service:

           dnf install autofs
           echo "/shares /etc/auto.shares" > /etc/auto.master.d/shares.autofs
           echo "work -rw nfs.lab.example.com:/exports/shared" > /etc/auto.shares
           systemctl enable --now autofs
           ls /shares/work

FILES
       /etc/auto.master, /etc/auto.master.d/*.autofs
              The master map.

       /etc/autofs.conf
              Settings, such as the idle time before an unmount.

NOTES
       The base directory (/shares above) belongs to autofs: do not create the
       key directories in it yourself. They appear when they are used, so ls
       /shares may look empty until you name one.

SEE ALSO
       auto.master(5), autofs(5), mount(8), nfs(5)
